WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.430
On one side, there’s the Jedi.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.270
On the other side, there’s the Sith.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.480
And in the middle, there’s Ahsoka Tano,
one of the most complex characters in Star

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:09.480
Wars.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.150
And if you want to know more about Anakin
Skywalker’s old padawan, then here’s the

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.499
untold truth of Ahsoka Tano.

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:19.780
When you get right down to it, the job of
a Jedi Knight is essentially half wandering

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:22.250
samurai-peacekeeper and half teacher.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:26.430
The basic idea is that after being certified
by the intergalactic school board that is

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:31.250
the Jedi Council, each Jedi Master takes on
an apprentice of their own, passing down their

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.080
knowledge and experience to the next generation.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.890
Ideally, this is done after the apprentice,
called a "padawan," gets a basic education

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:42.860
in the Force as a child, but there's, of course,
the odd exception.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:46.730
Either way, once the apprentice becomes a
full-fledged knight, they take on their own

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.690
apprentice, and the cycle continues down through
the ages.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.100
We know all this because we see it happening
in the prequel trilogy, in which Obi-Wan Kenobi

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:58.920
goes from being an apprentice to Qui-Gon Jinn
to a master in his own right who demands the

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.440
opportunity to train Anakin Skywalker.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.319
The thing is, we also see Anakin himself graduate
from padawan to knight in the prequel trilogy.

00:01:06.319 --> 00:01:10.619
So, if Anakin didn't go straight from being
Obi-Wan's student to becoming Darth Vader,

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:12.530
did he have an apprentice of his own?

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.810
It turns out he did, and she's one of the
most interesting characters in Star Wars history.

00:01:18.359 --> 00:01:22.959
In 2008, Star Wars: The Clone Wars was released
in theaters as a lead-in to a television series

00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:25.170
of the same name on Cartoon Network.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:29.509
Set between Attack of the Clones and Revenge
of the Sith, the film followed Obi-Wan Kenobi

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:34.439
and Anakin Skywalker as they attempted to
rescue Jabba the Hutt's son, Rotta, during

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:35.869
the eponymous conflict.

00:01:35.869 --> 00:01:40.009
When they got that assignment, Obi-Wan and
Anakin, along with the audience, were introduced

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:43.779
to Ahsoka Tano, who'd been assigned as Anakin's
brand new padawan.

00:01:43.779 --> 00:01:45.849
"Sir, I thought you said you'd never have
a padawan."

00:01:45.849 --> 00:01:48.770
"There's been a mix-up, the youngling isn't
with me."

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:53.670
Originally, however, the concept of The Clone
Wars was very different, and so was Ahsoka.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:57.849
When the show was first in development, in
fact, she wasn't even "Ahsoka" at all.

00:01:57.849 --> 00:02:01.810
The earliest concept from Dave Filoni — a
veteran animator who would go on to be the

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:06.329
supervising director, executive producer,
and a writer on the series — was built on

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:07.899
a very different idea.

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:12.140
The focus was on a ragtag crew working on
the fringes of the galaxy, dealing with the

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:16.500
sketchier side of the Star Wars universe,
far away from where Obi-Wan and Anakin were

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.540
fighting alongside the clone troopers.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.269
The Jedi would've been represented, though,
in the form of a master named Sendak and his

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:24.569
young apprentice, Ashla.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:28.900
Both Filoni and George Lucas wanted to have
a prominent female Jedi in order to appeal

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.459
to the girls in the audience, so when the
series was retooled to be built around Obi-Wan

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:36.629
and Anakin's time in the war, Ashla stuck
around.

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:40.680
Just like in Filoni's original pitch, she
was the viewpoint character who, like the

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.720
audience, was thrown into this legendary era
when the galaxy was barreling toward tragedy.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.110
The only difference was that unlike Ashla
herself, the audience already knew how Anakin's

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:55.019
story would end, giving the Clone Wars series
— and Ashla herself — a suspenseful tension

00:02:55.019 --> 00:02:56.310
that it would've otherwise lacked.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.260
Star Wars has never really been subtle about
character names.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:05.580
"Vader," for example, literally means "father"
in Dutch, and even if you can get past George

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.879
Lucas naming his chosen one character "Luke
S.," there's the fact that "Luke" is probably

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:12.049
related to the Latin word for light.

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:14.140
"Ashla" is at least a little different.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.049
In George Lucas' original notes for The Star
Wars, it was the proper name for the light

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:23.000
side of the Force, and if you were the kind
of fan who obsesses over the most minor details,

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.629
you might've seen it elsewhere, too.

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:29.049
While it's not spoken in the film, it's the
canonical name of one of Yoda's "youngling"

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:33.430
students briefly seen in Attack of the Clones,
who just happens to be a young Togruta who

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.989
bears a suspicious resemblance to Anakin's
future padawan.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:41.569
It's possible that they were meant to be the
same character, but in 2008, Leland Chee,

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:45.859
the guy officially in charge of maintaining
continuity for the saga, mentioned that they

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:48.500
couldn't be the same because their ages didn't
match up.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:53.659
In addition to the tweaks to her name, Ahsoka's
development also involved some pretty heavy

00:03:53.659 --> 00:03:55.040
visual designs.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.469
One of her earliest designs included high-heeled
boots and a pleated skirt that was somewhere

00:03:59.469 --> 00:04:04.170
between ballerina and cheerleader — not
exactly a bad look, but maybe not what you

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:08.910
want for the first female Jedi to ever have
a main character role in a Star Wars movie.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.939
In what might be the least surprising development
in the history of the franchise, her redesign

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:17.080
was heavily influenced by samurai and other
bits of Japanese culture.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:21.461
According to Filoni, the outfit she wears
in The Clone Wars was inspired by San, the

00:04:21.461 --> 00:04:25.740
wolf-riding heroine of Hayao Miyazaki's Princess
Mononoke.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.110
There's another specific influence, too.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:32.551
Unlike most of the other Jedi, Ahsoka holds
her lightsaber in the "reverse grip," with

00:04:32.551 --> 00:04:33.840
the blade pointed down.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.130
It's a cool look that sets her apart from
Anakin and Obi-Wan, and it was lifted from

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.790
the blind swordsman Zatoichi, from the long-running
series of films.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:47.640
In the opening scenes of The Clone Wars, Ahsoka
Tano arrives on the planet Christophsis, announcing

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.980
herself as Anakin's new apprentice on orders
from Yoda.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:55.700
This comes as news to Anakin, who definitely
doesn't want to take on a padawan of his own,

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.170
and he initially tries to pawn her off on
Obi-Wan.

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:02.130
To make matters worse, there's initially a
lot of friction between the two characters.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:07.670
Ahsoka is impatient, headstrong, occasionally
overconfident, and quick with a snarky retort,

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:10.450
which quickly earns her the nickname "Snips"
from Anakin.

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.580
In other words, she's a lot like Anakin himself,
who now begins to understand how annoying

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:15.580
that is.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:20.110
"Master, if you've taught me one thing, it's
that nothing is easy when you're around."

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:24.261
And as the story of Clone Wars goes on, it
becomes clear that that's the point, both

00:05:24.261 --> 00:05:26.170
for the audience and for the characters.

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:30.720
Yoda, who was always pretty reluctant to let
Anakin join up with the Jedi from the beginning,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.690
was clearly hoping that being put in the role
of a mentor would help to temper Anakin's

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:36.850
own reckless, hotheaded impulses.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.300
Sadly, as you might already know, that didn't
really work out.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.500
The relationship between the two characters,
however, develops into one of the most compelling

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.660
teacher-student dynamics that the entire Star
Wars saga has ever seen.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.960
By the end of the first act of the Clone Wars
movie, Anakin has recognized a lot of himself

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.020
in Ahsoka, and he clearly wants to give her
the kind of training that he wishes he would've

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.210
gotten from the more traditional, restrictive
Obi-Wan.

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.460
The only problem was that we already knew
that Anakin's own training would turn out

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:07.280
to be a failure, making it a pretty safe bet
that Ahsoka's would as well.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.480
The question was what form that failure would
take.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.090
We knew from the start of things that for
all of its fun, pulpy adventure, Ahsoka's

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:16.280
story was never going to have a happy ending.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.490
Before she'd shown up on the screen, we'd
seen Anakin personally slaughtering children

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.620
with his lightsaber as part of his fall from
grace.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:27.860
The Clone Wars — ostensibly a show directed
at children — forced its audience to ask

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:32.430
whether we would see Anakin turn on Ahsoka
and provide a permanent reason why she wasn't

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.390
hanging around with the Rebellion in the original
trilogy.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:36.490
The worst part?

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:40.280
Ahsoka finding herself on the wrong end of
Darth Vader's lightsaber seemed like it might

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.050
be the best-case scenario.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:45.900
The other most likely alternative was that
she would follow him in his fall, giving in

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:48.160
to her own reckless, hot-tempered nature.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.930
After all, now that we knew Anakin had an
apprentice, it wasn't out of the question

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:54.280
that we'd find out Vader had one, too.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:57.560
Fortunately for Ahsoka, neither of those fates
were hers.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.990
The way for Ahsoka to get around the fact
that there weren't a whole lot of Jedi hanging

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:05.450
around after the purge in Revenge of the Sith,
without being killed by her former mentor,

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:06.840
was actually pretty simple.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:12.130
If Order 66 was targeting the Jedi, then all
she had to do was just not be a Jedi anymore.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:16.190
The downside was that while it ultimately
saved her life, her parting with the Jedi

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.130
order wasn't exactly her idea.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:22.831
Instead, it came as part of an elaborate plot
to discredit both Ahsoka and the Jedi Order

00:07:22.831 --> 00:07:27.380
itself that played out in the fifth season
of The Clone Wars animated series.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:32.100
After the hangar at the Jedi Temple is bombed,
Ahsoka is framed for the murder of the bomber,

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.600
and then she's further framed for being the
mastermind behind it all.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.420
Even worse, part of the plot involves breaking
her out of prison, killing a whole lot of

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:44.090
clone troopers with her lightsaber in the
process, and framing her for all that as well.

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:47.810
In the end, it turns out that it actually
was a Jedi who bombed the temple and committed

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.790
the murders — it just wasn't Ahsoka.

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:54.550
It was Barriss Offee, a longtime member of
the Jedi Order who specialized in healing

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:59.080
and who'd lost her faith in the Order's commitment
to peace when they effectively became a military

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.020
force during the Clone Wars.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.470
The reveal is a shock to the other Jedi, not
only because Offee was thought of as a dependable,

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:09.300
well-liked Jedi, but because Ahsoka considered
her a close friend.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.310
She'd even turned to her for help when she
was trying to clear her name.

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:16.760
Despite the fact that she's able to clear
her name, Ahsoka is left with some pretty

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.820
hard feelings for the rest of the Order.

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:23.260
Not only had she been put on trial for a crime
she didn't commit, but the Jedi had sent both

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:28.550
Anakin Skywalker and Plo Koon, the Jedi who
first mentored her as a child, to apprehend

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:29.550
her.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.330
At this point, Ahsoka is left feeling personally
betrayed by those who were closest to her,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.930
who seemed awfully quick to believe she was
capable of horrible crimes.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:41.390
As a result, Ahsoka leaves the Jedi Order
after her trial, and while it's under bitter

00:08:41.390 --> 00:08:44.280
circumstances, it's also what saves her life.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.740
Since she isn't fighting alongside the clone
soldiers when Order 66 comes down, she isn't

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:51.960
a victim of the initial purge that kills off
most of the Jedi.

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:56.320
Instead, she escapes, living in hiding under
the name "Ashla" in the first years of the

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:57.320
Empire.

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:01.870
Eventually, though, Ahsoka realizes that staying
out of the conflict is only helping the Empire

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:07.150
remain in power, and she joins up with the
fledgling rebel movement started by Bail Organa

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:09.410
and adopts the codename "Fulcrum."

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:13.920
It's during this time that she winds up discovering
that Darth Vader isn't Anakin's murderer but

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.720
Anakin himself, who's still holding a grudge
against Ahsoka for abandoning his friendship

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:20.010
when she left the Order.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:24.000
When they finally fight, it seems like Vader
is going to strike her down just as he had

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.640
the others, but Ahsoka does something pretty
unexpected.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:31.460
She gets pulled through a portal into a "World
Between Worlds" and emerges two years later

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:32.900
after traveling through time.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:33.900
You know.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:34.900
Star Wars stuff.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:36.360
"Does this always happen to you?"

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:38.540
“Everywhere I go."

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:43.240
In the binary world of light and dark that
is the Star Wars saga, Ahsoka is fairly unique

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:47.200
in that she's a powerful Force user who's
neither Jedi nor Sith.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.960
That was reflected visually as her character
evolved over the years, too.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:54.740
Rather than sticking with the traditional
lightsaber colors — red for the bad guys

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:59.010
and pretty much every other color for the
heroes — the blades of Ahsoka's sabers are

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:00.010
white.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:03.330
The in-canon reason for this is that after
losing the sabers she used as a Jedi when

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:07.830
she left the Order, Ahsoka made new ones using
crystals that she'd taken from the red lightsabers

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:09.040
of a Sith inquisitor.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:14.470
Originally, the crystals were used in a traditionally
evil-colored red saber, but Ahsoka was able

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:18.980
to purify them, removing the taint of the
Dark Side and creating the distinctive white

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.480
blades that she used in her battles against
the Sith.

